## Chapter 33: File & OS Operations

File and operating system interaction is the bread and butter of systems programming. In Zig, `std.fs` provides a cohesive, cross-platform API that exposes the real capabilities of the underlying OS without burying them under layers of abstraction. This chapter covers file I/O, directory manipulation, path handling, environment variables, and process spawning. By the end, you will not only understand the `std.fs` module but also be able to build robust command-line tools that feel like first-class citizens on any platform.

---

### 1. Basic Usage

Every file operation starts with a **directory handle**, represented by `std.fs.Dir`. The most common starting points are `std.fs.cwd()` for the current working directory, and `std.fs.openDirAbsolute` (or `cwd().openDir`) for navigating to other directories.

#### Opening and Creating Files

```zig
const std = @import("std");

pub fn main() !void {
    const allocator = std.heap.page_allocator;

    // Open for reading
    const file = try std.fs.cwd().openFile("input.txt", .{});
    defer file.close();

    // Open for writing (create or truncate)
    const out_file = try std.fs.cwd().createFile("output.txt", .{ .read = false });
    defer out_file.close();

    // Read entire file into allocated buffer
    const contents = try file.readToEndAlloc(allocator, 1024 * 1024 * 100); // 100 MB limit
    defer allocator.free(contents);
    _ = contents;

    // Write bytes
    try out_file.writeAll("Hello, Zig!\n");
}
```

The `.{}` flags argument to `openFile` and `createFile` uses default permissions and exclusive access if not specified. Use `.{}` for a read‑only open; for write‑only, you often want `.{}` as well (but `createFile` is write‑only by default). More sophisticated modes like `openFile` with `.mode = .read_write` are available. Under Windows, `createFile` can take `share_mode` flags; on Unix, the default mode is `0o666` modified by `umask`, but you can pass `.mode` to set it explicitly.

#### Reading Line by Line

Zig’s buffered I/O is opt‑in. To read a text file line by line, wrap the file’s `reader()` in a `std.io.BufferedReader` and use a `std.io.fixedBufferStream`:

```zig
pub fn readLines(allocator: std.mem.Allocator, path: []const u8) !void {
    const file = try std.fs.cwd().openFile(path, .{});
    defer file.close();

    var buf_reader = std.io.bufferedReader(file.reader());
    const reader = buf_reader.reader();
    var line_buf: [4096]u8 = undefined;

    while (try reader.readUntilDelimiterOrEof(&line_buf, '\n')) |line| {
        // line is a slice into line_buf; copy it if you need ownership
        const owned = try allocator.dupe(u8, line);
        defer allocator.free(owned);
        // process line...
        _ = owned;
    }
}
```

For the common case of reading all lines into an `ArrayList` of strings, you can use `readUntilDelimiterAlloc` or write a small helper.

#### Directory Traversal

`std.fs.Dir` exposes an **iterator** that yields `std.fs.Dir.Entry` structs, containing the entry’s name (a slice into a provided buffer) and its `Kind` (file, directory, symlink, etc.).

```zig
pub fn listDir(path: []const u8) !void {
    var dir = try std.fs.cwd().openDir(path, .{ .iterate = true });
    defer dir.close();

    var it = dir.iterate();
    while (try it.next()) |entry| {
        std.debug.print("{s}: {s}\n", .{ @tagName(entry.kind), entry.name });
    }
}
```

The iterator reuses an internal buffer, so no allocation occurs unless you copy the name. If you need to store names, `dupe` them with an allocator.

#### Path Manipulation

The `std.fs.path` namespace provides functions that work with raw `[]const u8` slices, never allocating (except for `join` variants that need an allocator). It is fully cross‑platform:

```zig
const base = "/home/user";
const file = "docs/readme.txt";
const full = try std.fs.path.join(allocator, &.{ base, file });
defer allocator.free(full);
// full is "/home/user/docs/readme.txt" on Unix, "C:\home\user\docs\readme.txt" on Windows
```

Other important functions:
- `std.fs.path.dirname` and `basename`
- `std.fs.path.extension`
- `std.fs.path.isAbsolute`
- `std.fs.path.resolve` (allocates)
- `std.fs.path.sep` (platform‑appropriate separator)

#### Environment Variables and Process Spawning

Access environment variables with `std.process.getEnvVarOwned` (allocating) or `std.os.getenv` (returns null‑terminated pointer, can fail).

```zig
const home = try std.process.getEnvVarOwned(allocator, "HOME");
defer allocator.free(home);
```

Spawning a child process is done with `std.process.Child`. This struct allows setting the executable, arguments, environment, working directory, and I/O redirection.

```zig
pub fn runCommand(allocator: std.mem.Allocator, argv: []const []const u8) !void {
    var child = std.process.Child.init(argv, allocator);
    child.stdout_behavior = .Pipe;
    child.stderr_behavior = .Pipe;

    try child.spawn();

    const stdout = child.stdout.?.reader().readAllAlloc(allocator, 1024 * 1024);
    const stderr = child.stderr.?.reader().readAllAlloc(allocator, 1024 * 1024);
    defer allocator.free(stdout);
    defer allocator.free(stderr);

    const term = try child.wait();
    std.debug.print("Exit code: {d}\nstdout: {s}\nstderr: {s}\n", .{ term.ExitCode, stdout, stderr });
}
```

The `init` function takes a slice of arguments where `argv[0]` is the executable path; `allocator` is used only for the environment block if needed.

